Subject: Quick fixes for the AI Triage Flow!

Hi Jennifer,

Great job getting the AI Builder integrated! I ran a deep-dive test on the flow
today and found out why the sentiment and urgency weren't being captured
correctly.

It turns out the Run a prompt action was accidentally using Microsoft's default
"AI sentiment" system prompt. Because of that, the AI was ignoring our custom
instructions and just returning the word "Negative," which broke the data
mapping. We also need to add a quick "Unpacking" step so SharePoint can actually
read the AI's data.

Here is the step-by-step guide to swap this out and get it working perfectly:

Step 1: Delete the incorrect AI action

1.  Open your flow in Edit mode.
2.  Find your Run a prompt action, click the three dots (...), and select
    Delete. (Don't worry if the steps below it show an error temporarily).

Step 2: Add the correct Custom Prompt

1.  Click the + icon directly below your Html to text step.
2.  Search for Run a prompt and add the AI Builder action.
3.  In the parameters on the left, click the Prompt dropdown.
4.  Look under your custom/shared prompts and select MI Triage Extractor Prompt.
5.  An EmailBody box will appear. Click inside it, click the dynamic content
    lightning bolt, and select The plain text content (the green icon from the
    Html to Text step). Note: Make sure you use this plain text, not the raw
    Outlook Body!

Step 3: Unpack the AI Data (Parse JSON)

Now we have to translate the AI's output into usable variables.

1.  Click the + icon right below your new Run a prompt action.
2.  Search for and add Parse JSON (Data Operation).
3.  Content: Click the lightning bolt and select Response (or Text) from the new
    Run a prompt step.
4.  Schema: Click the "Generate from sample" button, paste the exact block of
    code below, and click Done:

{
  "Is_Spam": false,
  "Loan_Number": "123456",
  "Extracted_Date": "null",
  "Sentiment": "Routine Request",
  "Financial_Risk": false
}

(Crucial tweak: Once generated, look at the schema code. Change the "type" for
Loan_Number and Extracted_Date from "string" to ["string", "null"] so the flow
doesn't crash if the AI leaves them blank).

Step 4: Upgrade your Spam Condition

The AI is now doing the spam filtering for us, so we can delete the old
hardcoded rules!

1.  Go to your Condition box (Step 7).
2.  Delete all those old rules (Subject contains out of office, etc.).
3.  Replace it with one single rule: Is_Spam (from your new Parse JSON step) is
    equal to true (use the fx expression tab to type the word true).

Step 5: Map the Data to SharePoint

Finally, let's plug the AI's data into your ticket!

1.  Open your SharePoint Create_item action.
2.  Map Loan Number, Sentiment, and Due Date to the new dynamic variables
    popping out of your Parse JSON step.
3.  For the Priority column, instead of choosing 'Normal', click the fx
    (Expression) icon and paste this exact formula to auto-flag high-risk
    tickets: if(outputs('Parse_JSON')?['body/Financial_Risk'], 'Critical',
    'Routine')

Hit Save and give it a test run with a dummy "Urgent" email. Let me know if you
run into any snags!

